12. You are a public body under the Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act (FOIPPA). What are your obligations under the Act? You must:

- make your records available to the public;

- protect the privacy of individuals; and

- appoint a designated officer within your organization to be responsible for administering the Act. 13. Do I have to pay a fee to make a request for government records? Yes. The Act allows public bodies to charge a fee to make photocopies or, in some cases, for time spent on the request. 14. Do I have to pay a fee to make an access to information request? No. The Act does not allow public bodies to charge a fee for the time spent processing an access to information request. However, you may have to pay a fee for photocopies or for a computer disk. 15. How do I make a request for government records? You must make a written request to the public body. You must provide your name and contact information and describe the records you are requesting. 16. What happens after I make a request for government records? The public body has 30 days to respond to your request. If the public body needs more time, it may extend the time limit by another 30 days. If the public body denies your request, it must provide a written explanation for the denial. 17. Can I appeal a decision made by a public body under the Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act? Yes. If you are not satisfied with the decision made by the public body, you can appeal the decision to the Information and Privacy Commissioner. The Commissioner will review the decision and make a determination. 18. What is the role of the Information and Privacy Commissioner? The Information and Privacy Commissioner is responsible for administering and enforcing the Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act. The Commissioner's role includes:

- investigating complaints;

- providing guidance to public bodies on their obligations under the Act;

- conducting audits of public bodies to ensure compliance with the Act; and

- making recommendations to the government on changes to the Act. 19. How can I contact the Information and Privacy Commissioner? You can contact the Information and Privacy Commissioner by phone, email, or mail. The contact information is available on the Commissioner's website.

